<div style="text-align: center; background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Dream Jobs – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Dream Jobs</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> What Are the Jobs of the Future?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Communication: Assist in negotiations between student groups. Text: Mediate information from notices or announcements for broader understanding.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can assist groups negotiating job ideas by simplifying phrases (e.g., "Can we pick one dream job to research?" while showing job options like "doctor, teacher, artist" on a chart). Can mediate information from job announcements with visuals (e.g., pointing to job descriptions like "Skills needed: computer skills" in a flyer). Can explain terms from job notices, using examples (e.g., "Requires teamwork" by saying, "You work with others," and pointing to a teamwork image).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f0f0f0; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f0f0f0; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f0f0f0; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by displaying images of various future jobs (e.g., AI specialist, remote health consultant, drone operator). Students quickly brainstorm (in pairs) one key skill needed for each job. The teacher asks, "What careers do you think will be in demand in 20 years?" (Pragmatic focus). Students share ideas, linking to previously learned vocabulary like 'automation,' 'technology,' 'adaptability.' This activity reviews previously learned vocabulary and prepares students for discussing the Future Job Poster project.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher explains the concept of "mediating" by showing a complex job description (e.g., "Requires advanced proficiency in synergistic cross-functional collaboration tools") and then simplifying it: "This means you'll need to work well with others and use computer programs for teamwork." The teacher models the pronunciation of 'collaboration' and 'adaptability,' focusing on stress patterns. (Pronunciation focus: multisyllabic words).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I mediate information, do I make it more complicated or easier to understand?" (Expected answer: Easier.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"When would it be useful to simplify information for someone?" (Expected answer: When they don't understand, when they speak a different language, for a younger audience.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">[Approx. 7-10 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Dream Jobs".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a simplified example of a "Future Job Poster" from a fictional student group, highlighting some key terms related to future jobs and skills (e.g., "AI development," "remote work opportunities," "adaptability in the workforce," "critical thinking skills"). The poster also includes predictions using future simple and future continuous (e.g., "New jobs will emerge in technology fields," "In ten years, people will be working more remotely"). Some terms on the poster are deliberately slightly more complex than B1.1 to necessitate mediation practice.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Mediate information from notices or announcements for broader understanding. Assist in negotiations by clarifying terms.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions:</b> "What are two main types of future jobs predicted on this poster?" "What essential skill is mentioned for almost all future careers?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match challenging terms from the poster (e.g., 'automation,' 'advancement,' 'adaptability') with a simpler explanation provided by the teacher on a handout.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">[Approx. 8-10 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Practicing mediation by simplifying complex language related to future jobs. Using future simple (predictions), future continuous (long-term goals), and modals (possibilities). Vocabulary: 'automation,' 'adaptability,' 'remote work,' 'AI (artificial intelligence),' 'demand,' 'evolve,' 'acquire,' 'require.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled practice – 'Simplify This!':</b> The teacher provides sentences from a mock job announcement or the example poster containing B1.1-level vocabulary but slightly complex phrasing. Students work individually to rewrite or verbally rephrase these sentences using simpler words and structures. E.g., "This role will entail significant innovation and require continuous skill acquisition" becomes "This job will need new ideas, and you will learn new skills all the time." (Grammar: future simple, future continuous, modals).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled – 'Explain to a Friend':</b> In pairs, students receive cards with one specific term or phrase related to future jobs (e.g., 'digital nomad,' 'gig economy,' 'upskilling,' 'sustainable energy sector'). One student picks a card and must explain the term to their partner using simpler language and an example, without using the exact words on the card. The partner provides feedback on clarity. The teacher circulates, checking for accurate use of vocabulary and pronunciation, specifically /dʒ/ in "change" and /ʒ/ in "vision" if those words arise (Pronunciation focus: contrastive sounds).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair interaction – 'Poster Clarification':</b> Students refer to their own "Future Job Poster" drafts (or the example poster). In pairs, they take turns acting as a "mediator." Student A points to a concept or term on the poster (e.g., "the impact of automation on employment") and Student B, acting as a mediator, explains it clearly and simply to Student A. Student A then asks a follow-up question, and Student B uses relevant vocabulary (e.g., 'workforce,' 'replace') and future tenses to elaborate. They switch roles. (Sociolinguistic focus: adapting tone to clarify for a peer).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">[Approx. 15-18 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Dream Jobs".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative "Future Job Poster" Mediation & Negotiation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in their established groups to finalize their "Future Job Poster." The core of this stage is *mediating* information within their group and preparing to mediate it for others. Each group needs to ensure every member understands all aspects of their poster. They will also practice mediating potential "negotiations" or disagreements. For example, if two group members want to feature different "in-demand industries," a third member will mediate to find a compromise, simplifying each argument and proposing a solution (e.g., "So, Juan, you think sustainable energy will be the most important. And Maria, you predict AI will have more opportunities. Maybe we can include both, or pick one dream job from each field to research?"). They use pragmatic functions like "What careers do you think will be in demand?" and "I will be an engineer and will work on innovative projects." as they discuss their poster content. (Sociolinguistic focus: adapting language choices based on the audience within the group).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in explaining and simplifying poster content, collaborative interaction in group decision-making, effective mediation of ideas, and clear communication using target vocabulary and grammatical structures (future simple, future continuous, modals).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">[Approx. 10-12 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-assessment.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Groups present their finalized "Future Job Poster" to another group. During this presentation, each member must take turns explaining at least one aspect of the poster (e.g., a specific future job, a skill, a trend like 'remote work,' or the impact of 'automation'). The focus of the assessment is on their ability to *mediate* this information by simplifying terms, giving examples, and ensuring the listening group understands. The teacher observes if they can assist in clarifying and ensuring understanding, and if they effectively mediated any internal group discussions. They should explicitly use future simple and continuous to describe predictions and long-term goals. (e.g., "This job will be in high demand because technology will continue to advance rapidly.")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of explanation and simplification of complex terms.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Appropriate use of target vocabulary (e.g., 'adaptability,' 'innovation,' 'workforce,' 'prediction').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of future simple and future continuous for predictions and goals.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation, especially of multisyllabic words (e.g., "automation," "advancement").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Evidence of assisting in group negotiation or clarifying ideas for peers.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of our Future Job Poster did I explain most clearly to the other group? Why?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How did I help my group understand a difficult idea or agree on a specific job for our poster?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">[Approx. 5-7 min]</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Student and teacher reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                    <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What did I find challenging about mediating information or ideas during our poster project, and how can I improve my communication skills?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How has working on the Future Job Poster helped me understand the skills and adaptability needed for my own career goals?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What worked well in facilitating student mediation and understanding of future job concepts? Were students able to effectively simplify complex information and assist in group negotiations? What adjustments or additional scaffolds might be needed for students who struggled with explaining or clarifying? Did the activity effectively integrate all target linguistic competences? I noticed students used 'innovation' and 'adaptability' frequently, but sometimes struggled with stress in 'artificial intelligence.'</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson concludes the "Dream Jobs / What Are the Jobs of the Future?" theme and the 21st Century Skills project. The next sequence of lessons will introduce a new theme, building on communication skills developed in this unit, perhaps focusing on ethical considerations of new technologies or global collaboration in the workforce.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">[Approx. 3-5 min]</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>